  "why": "The statement asks whether a single t makes every t-tough graph Hamiltonian. A result proving that a threshold exists within a graph class (chordal, (P4∪P1)-free, (2P2∪P1)-free, (P2∪kP1)-free, (P3∪kP1)-free) settles a restriction of that question, so I scored these as case. For the 2k-connected forbidden-subgraph results I also scored case, because k-tough already implies 2k-connected, so each still gives a threshold for its class. The planar work at toughness exactly 3/2 does not ask whether a threshold exists: Tutte's planar threshold was already known. That work, the degree-sequence and Ore-type conditions, the fractional-Hamiltonicity result and the circumference results all answer different questions, so I scored them variant. Bauer–Broersma–Veldman moves the lower bound on t to 9/4, so it is a bound. Nikoghosyan does not move that bound, so it is none. The Tanyel thesis was approved by a named committee, which I counted as digestion (F9). It repeats the 2025 preprint's theorem, so I kept it as case. I answered the AI role for each event from its own quote. All AI-assisted events are variants. The two Segal–Verstraete papers are borderline between assisted and none, because the model only suggested strategies. I scored them assisted because the record says the suggestions (elimination trees, weighted sums) went into the proofs.",
